Design for Upcycling strategy seeks to find new value by transforming, repurposing or refashioning waste or used materials and products. Here, the designer should encourage the users to maintain the product even after becomes obsolete, failed, broken, cracked or worn-out. In the Design for Maintenance strategy, upcycling is a creative act which is performed usually by individuals, artists and makers. However, it shouldn't be confused with the industrial upcycling, a process that is more similar to recycling and that involves a complex material transformation. Designers need to make a distinction between the Design for Upcycling under the Maintenance (X1) from Design for Upcycling under the Reuse (X1).  The difference lies in the fact that, in the Maintenance, it is the owner of the product who upcycling the product, while in the Reuse it is someone else ( artist, industry, etc.). Another consideration to make is the difference between Design for Upcycling from Design for Repairing. In the latter, the function of the product is restored, while in the upcycling the product is somehow renovated and the function and purpose change.